  "u1-ns8": passage("u1-ns8", "Kings, lords, and peasants in western Europe",
    "In western Europe around 1200, kings shared power with nobles, who held land as fiefs in return for military service, and with the Church. Most people were peasants on manors, and serfs owed labor and dues to their lords and could not leave the land without permission. Over the next two centuries, some kings built stronger states. In England, barons forced King John to accept Magna Carta in 1215, and later kings called Parliaments, which included knights and representatives of towns, to approve new taxes. In France, kings such as Philip II and Philip IV added to the royal lands, built up a corps of paid officials, and taxed the clergy. Farming improved with the heavy plow and the three-field system, the population grew, and towns and merchant guilds multiplied. Wars such as the Hundred Years' War between England and France (1337 to 1453) pushed kings to raise larger armies and new taxes."),
  "u1-ns9": passage("u1-ns9", "Ruling large states, c. 1200 to c. 1450",
    "States in different regions faced the same problem: how to rule large territories and many peoples. The Mali Empire left conquered chiefs in place as long as they paid tribute and sent soldiers, and its kings' Islamic faith tied them to North African merchants and scholars, while most villagers kept older beliefs. The Mexica of central Mexico also left conquered city-states their own rulers but demanded heavy tribute in goods such as cotton cloth, cacao, and feathers, and they built no large administration to govern them. The Inca, by contrast, governed directly. They divided their empire into provinces under Inca officials, moved whole communities to new regions, built some 40,000 kilometers of roads, and required labor service, called mit'a, from every household. Song China ruled through salaried officials chosen by examination, and the Delhi Sultans ruled a mostly Hindu population through Muslim military commanders who collected the land tax."),
  "u1-ns10": passage("u1-ns10", "The Delhi Sultanate",
    "The Delhi Sultanate, founded in 1206, was ruled by a series of Turkic and Afghan dynasties until 1526. Its sultans were Muslims ruling a mostly Hindu population. They granted military commanders the right to collect revenue from assigned lands, called iqtas, in return for keeping troops, and they collected the jizya from many non-Muslims, though Hindu landholders and officials remained important in the countryside. The sultanate turned back several Mongol invasions in the late 1200s and early 1300s, and Alauddin Khalji (ruled 1296 to 1316) fixed the prices of grain and other goods in Delhi to feed his large army. Under Muhammad bin Tughluq (ruled 1325 to 1351), who employed the traveler Ibn Battuta as a judge, the sultanate reached its greatest size, but his attempts to move the capital south and to issue copper tokens in place of silver coins failed. After Timur sacked Delhi in 1398, the sultanate lost most of its provinces to regional kingdoms."),
  "u1-ns11": passage("u1-ns11", "Song technology",
    "The Song period was one of the most inventive in Chinese history. Woodblock printing, already used under the Tang, spread widely, and in the 1040s a craftsman named Bi Sheng made movable type from baked clay. Sailors used the magnetic compass at sea by about 1100. Gunpowder, first made by Chinese alchemists, was used in Song armies in fire arrows, bombs, and early fire lances against the Jurchen and the Mongols. Kilns at Jingdezhen and elsewhere produced fine porcelain for buyers at home and across Asia, and new strains of rice and better iron tools raised farm output. Many of these techniques later spread west along the Silk Roads and the sea routes."),
  "u1-ns12": passage("u1-ns12", "The Mexica state",
    "In 1428 the Mexica of Tenochtitlan joined the cities of Texcoco and Tlacopan in a Triple Alliance and began to conquer the peoples of central Mexico. By 1500 some thirty-eight tributary provinces sent goods to the alliance, among them maize, beans, cotton cloth, cacao, feathers, jade, and warriors' costumes, recorded by scribes in painted books. Long-distance merchants called pochteca traded far beyond the empire and also served as spies. Mexica religion taught that the gods needed human blood to keep the sun moving, and warfare supplied captives for sacrifice at the Templo Mayor, the great temple in the center of Tenochtitlan. The city itself, built on an island in Lake Texcoco and fed in part by raised fields called chinampas, may have held 200,000 people."),
